\section{Executable admission and CPU reference contract}\label{app:admission}
\textbf{Public inputs and interpretation.} A frozen manifest lists every relevant construction record, its unique ID and source text, a common currency, supplied private value and feasible menu. Record inclusion must be resolved from public information before evaluation. The CPU profile interprets only a whole-record field of the form \texttt{record\_id=<ID>; price\_to\_beat=<amount> <currency>}, with a finite nonnegative decimal amount in the declared currency. It performs no currency conversion or implicit interpretation of an opening bid, offer, superseded draft or withdrawal. Different wording, multiple amounts, missing units or extra prose remain unresolved. The declared source schema must actually identify the construction price for this task; syntactic acceptance alone cannot establish real-world factual truth.

\textbf{Admission and updates.} An extraction proposal must name a manifest ID and exact character offsets for the amount in that record. The verifier checks the entire source grammar, ID, currency, amount and offsets independently of the proposal, and admits the verified amount as a point interval. Schema-valid but numerically wrong proposals, hallucinated or duplicate IDs, unsupported spans and conflicting updates are logged and rejected. Unknown records retain $[0,+\infty)$ and stay in the fixed denominator. A repair batch is checked atomically against its declared chunk IDs; invalid batches preserve the prior intervals. Source text is immutable and never treated as an instruction. Accepted updates must be nonempty subsets of prior intervals. No hidden prices, scoring labels or claimed model confidence enter admission.

\textbf{Accounting and validation boundary.} The reference uses exact rational arithmetic, the stated inclusive price threshold, lowest-bid ties and fixed chunk IDs. Each attempted call reserves an output cap within the remaining total allowance and consumes a call slot before execution; failures retain their charged tokens and recorded stage time. No model, prompt adapter or GPU timing pilot is supplied by this reference. Finite fixtures cover invalid manifests, unsupported amounts, stale spans, unit conflicts, nonnested updates, cap exhaustion and conditional action bounds. They are engineering checks, not independent extraction, coverage, utility or runtime results. Future evaluation must keep parser successes and failures and audit source-schema validity separately from numeric extraction correctness. The existing development traces use a different record-language protocol and cannot validate this profile without a separately frozen adapter and independent cases.

